20"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Mallet</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Beltr&#x000e1;n</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Neukirchen</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Linares</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><article-title>Natural hybridization in Heliconiine butterflies: the species boundary is a continuum</article-title><source>BMC Evol Biol</source><year>2007</year><volume>7</volume><fpage>28</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1471-2148-7-28</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17319954</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B21"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Beltr&#x000e1;n</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Jiggins</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name><name><surname>Brower</surname><given-names>AVZ</given-names></name><name><surname>Bermingham</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Mallet</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><article-title>Do pollen feeding and pupal-mating have a single origin in Heliconius? Inferences from multilocus sequence data </article-title><source>Biol J Linn Soc</source><year>2007</year><volume>92</volume><fpage>221</fpage><lpage>239</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1095-8312.2007.00830.x</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B22"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Linares</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><article-title>The ghost of mimicry past: laboratory reconstruction of an extinct butterfly "race"</article-title><source>Heredity</source><year>1997</year><volume>78</volume><fpage>628</fpage><lpage>635</lpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="B23"><mixed-citation publication-type="book"><name><surname>Gilbert</surname><given-names>LE</given-names></name><person-group person-group-type="editor">Boggs CL, Watt WB, Ehrlich PR</person-group><article-title>Adaptive novelty through introgr